### try / catch (error trap)

`try { body } catch e { handler }` is an EXPRESSION: it yields the
body's last value, or -- when the body raises a HARD error -- the
handler's, with `e` bound to the canonical error record
`{kind, message}` (dispatch on `e.kind`; kinds are stable kebab-case
tags like `shape`, `arity`, `runtime`). `err(...)` VALUES are data,
not hard errors: they flow through untouched. break/continue/return
signals also pass through. Design: docs/error-handling.md.

### `?` (Result propagation)
--- assert sigs ---
23:def u:assert_true(condition, message) {
41:def u:assert_eq(actual, expected, message) {
59:def u:assert_approx(actual, expected, tolerance, message) {
79:def u:assert_gt(actual, lower_bound, message) {
122:def u:assert_error_kind(error, expected, message) {
131:def u:assert_ok(result, message) {
140:def u:assert_err(result, message) {
122:def u:assert_error_kind(error, expected, message) {
123-  "Pass when a caught hard-error record has the expected textual kind.";
124-  if u:text_equal(error.kind, expected) {
125-    ok(1)
--- @test annotation fields ---
